    "faq_urfa": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_urfa": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Where do I find Urfa pepper, and what if I cannot?",
            "text": "<p>Middle Eastern and Turkish grocers carry it as <strong>isot</strong>, and most spice retailers sell it online. <strong>Aleppo flakes are the swap named in the recipe</strong> and are far easier to find &mdash; brighter and slightly hotter, but the same job. If all you have is standard crushed red pepper, use about a third as much and add a pinch of smoked paprika to replace the depth you are missing.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_temp": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why pull the salmon at 120&ndash;125&deg;F?",
            "text": "<p>Because it keeps climbing after it leaves the heat. A rested fillet gains roughly 5&deg;F, so 122&deg;F in the skillet lands near 127&deg;F on the plate &mdash; opaque, flaking, still glossy. Wait until the thermometer reads 130&deg;F before pulling and you will eat it at 135&deg;F, which is where the fat renders out and the texture turns chalky. Probe the thickest point at an angle from the side so you do not puncture the skin you just crisped.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_skin": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My skin never gets crisp. What am I doing wrong?",
            "text": "<p>Almost always moisture or movement. <strong>Pat the skin genuinely dry</strong> &mdash; drier than feels necessary &mdash; and get the ghee glossy before the fish goes in; a lukewarm pan steams the skin instead of searing it. Then press gently for the first 20&ndash;30 seconds so it does not curl away from the metal, and <strong>do not move the fillet</strong> for the next two to three minutes. Peeking is what tears the skin.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_warm": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make the farro salad ahead?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, and it is better for it. Cook the farro, load it and dress it with half the dressing while it is warm, then refrigerate &mdash; that warm-dressing step is the one thing you cannot do later. Up to three days. Hold back the avocado, the reserved dressing and the final pinch of Urfa until you plate it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_farro": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Pearled, semi-pearled or whole farro?",
            "text": "<p>The 25&ndash;30 minute timing here assumes <strong>pearled</strong>, which is what most shops sell. Semi-pearled takes closer to 35 minutes and whole farro wants an overnight soak and up to an hour. Whichever you buy, cook it in plenty of salted water like pasta and drain it &mdash; do not try to cook it in a measured amount of liquid.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia King Salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in its sealed pack, about 12 hours. In a hurry, put the sealed pack in a bowl of cold water for 20&ndash;30 minutes. Never at room temperature and never in the microwave. Then pat it dry properly before it goes anywhere near the skillet &mdash; a wet fillet will not take a sear.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_urfa",
        "q_temp",
        "q_skin",
        "q_warm",
        "q_farro",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
